            <ArticleTitle>Chronic paroxysmal hemicrania: dissociation of the pain and autonomic features.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>111-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The case of a woman suffering from chronic paroxysmal hemicrania is presented. Most attacks were unilateral and recurred on the same side. On a few occasions, attacks were observed on the contralateral side. In addition, the patient reported some incomplete attacks on the usually symptomatic side with autonomic phenomena, but without pain. That &quot;partial&quot; attacks would exist has been suspected on theoretical ground. This is, however, the first time such attacks have been reported by a patient. Therefore, a double dissociation of the symptomatology seemed to exist: (1) a side shift of attacks, and (2) incomplete (&quot;partial&quot;) attacks. These unexpected findings occurred after two indomethacin treatment withdrawals. A possible central and &quot;midline&quot; origin of attacks or an indomethacin after-effect or both are discussed as a likely explanation for such a dissociation of symptoms and signs.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
